findings.recommendation:
Log a login_failed event before throwing 429. The reason metadata should include which threshold tripped so the operator can distinguish per-email targeting from per-IP spraying:

private Mono<Void> checkBruteForce(String emailKey, String ipKey, String ip, String userAgent) {
    return Mono.zip(currentCount(emailKey), currentCount(ipKey))
            .flatMap(t -> {
                if (t.getT1() >= EMAIL_THRESHOLD || t.getT2() >= IP_THRESHOLD) {
                    String reason = t.getT1() >= EMAIL_THRESHOLD ? "brute_force_email" : "brute_force_ip";
                    eventService.logEvent(null, EVENT_LOGIN_FAILED, ip, userAgent, Map.of("reason", reason));
                    return Mono.<Void>error(AppException.tooManyRequests("Too many login attempts. Try again later."));
                }
                return Mono.<Void>empty();
            })
            .onErrorResume(...);  // existing fail-open
}

Add a unit test: AuthServiceTest::login_emailBruteForceLimitReached_logsBruteForceFailedEvent that asserts verify(eventService).logEvent(isNull(), eq("login_failed"), ..., eq(Map.of("reason", "brute_force_email"))).

Use Locale.ROOT explicitly:

  String email = request.getEmail() == null ? "" : request.getEmail().trim().toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT);

Apply the same Locale.ROOT to any other email lowercase() in the codebase (Tasks 5 registration, password reset). Add a unit test parametrised on Locale.forLanguageTag("tr-TR") (use Locale.setDefault inside the test, restore afterward) that asserts canonicalization is identical to en_US for an email containing 'I'.
